The times in columns D and J are stored as text (for example "Jan 5 2012 12:15:32.12"), so `D2-J2` would not subtract them directly. The last 11 characters of each string are the time with hundredths of a second (for example "12:15:32.12"). `TIMEVALUE` can convert that part. Multiplying by 86400 turns the difference into seconds. `ABS` is used to match the Cost Delta formula in N2.

This only compares the time of day, not the date. It assumes each matched pair falls on the same day, which is true in the sample rows.

{"formula": "=ABS(TIMEVALUE(RIGHT(D2,11))-TIMEVALUE(RIGHT(J2,11)))*86400"}

M4 should hold the Total Value for stock K4 between the From Date (H4) and To Date (I4). That means summing column F where the stock code matches K4 and the date falls between $H$4 and $I$4.

The ranges are absolute so the formula can be copied down to the other stock rows.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS($F$4:$F$36,$B$4:$B$36,K4,$A$4:$A$36,\">=\"&$H$4,$A$4:$A$36,\"<=\"&$I$4)"}

Accrued interest is the full coupon per period times the fraction of the current period that has elapsed since the last coupon. That fraction is days since the last coupon (`COUPDAYBS`) divided by days in the period (`COUPDAYS`). The formula uses basis `1`, the same as the YIELD, PRICE and COUP formulas in the other columns. Here the per-period coupon is face value × rate ÷ payments per year.

I avoided `ACCRINT` because by default it accrues from the issue date rather than from the last coupon.

{"formula": "=B2*H2/100/G2*COUPDAYBS(D2,F2,G2,1)/COUPDAYS(D2,F2,G2,1)"}

The grid in F1:H5 has quarters (1–4) down column F and years (2019, 2020) across row 1. Column C already holds the max price for each quarter's block of rows, so G2 should be the max price for quarter 1 of 2019.

I'll use MAXIFS with two date bounds: on or after the quarter's first day, and before the next quarter's first day. The references are mixed-absolute ($A$2:$A$45, $B$2:$B$45, G$1, $F2) so the formula can be filled across H and down to row 5.

{"formula": "=MAXIFS($B$2:$B$45,$A$2:$A$45,\">=\"&DATE(G$1,3*$F2-2,1),$A$2:$A$45,\"<\"&DATE(G$1,3*$F2+1,1))"}

J5 should hold the cheapest price for part number H5. I5 then uses that price to look up the person. A MIN over prices where B matches H5 works in older Excel without needing array entry, using the same nested-INDEX style as I5.

{"formula": "=MIN(INDEX(($B$3:$B$21=$H5)*$D$3:$D$21+($B$3:$B$21<>$H5)*1E+99,))"}

Simpler option: `=MINIFS($D$3:$D$21,$B$3:$B$21,$H5)`. The workbook already uses the INDEX workaround, but MINIFS is cleaner and fits most current Excel versions, so I'll go with it.

```json
{"formula": "=MINIFS($D$3:$D$21,$B$3:$B$21,$H5)"}
```

D14 sits under "Percentage by event", so I'm reading it as the average satisfaction percentage across the five events. Column C already gives the pooled percentage of individuals.

The event cells hold text like `9, 64.3%`, so the formula pulls out the percentage after the comma and converts it to a number. Blank cells (no responses in that category) count as 0%, and the total is divided by the 5 events. The `$B3:$F3` reference keeps the columns fixed but lets the row change, so it fills down to D15–D18 for the other rating levels.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(IFERROR(VALUE(MID($B3:$F3,FIND(\",\",$B3:$F3)+2,10)),0))/5"}

This takes the 14 days ending today from the "New" row and averages them. A6 holds today's day of the month. Row 3 has one column per day, with B3 as day 1. So OFFSET from A3 by `A6-13` columns lands on day `A6-13`. Taking 14 cells from there covers today and the 13 days before it. Dividing by 14 gives the daily run rate.

If today is before the 14th, the start position falls before day 1 and the formula returns a `#REF!` error.

{"formula": "=SUM(OFFSET(A3,0,A6-13,1,14))/14"}

Row 39 is a totals row, and the neighbouring cells use `=SUM(X8:X38)`. Column E follows the same pattern, so the formula sums E8:E38.

Every entry in column E is the text "<1", which SUM ignores, so this total will show 0.

{"formula": "=SUM(E8:E38)"}

C13 has `=IF(LH_OI="Yes",COUNTIF(LH_Sites,">0"),"")`, and the Community column uses `Community_OI`. B13 likely counts community units from the rent roll. The Community side (B14–B16) consistently gates on `Community_OI="Yes"`, so I'll mirror C13's structure with that flag.

The unit count source is uncertain. The best available reference is the 'Rent Roll' column O (unit type codes), which AL6 already counts with `'Rent Roll'!O3:O5000`. A plain COUNTA over that range gives the number of community units.

{"formula": "=IF(Community_OI=\"Yes\",COUNTA('Rent Roll'!O3:O5000),\"\")"}

The "Aug Sales Number" section counts customers by type for August. Column E (Month) holds the month number, so August is 8.

Counting "New" rows in B2:B26 gives 4 (rows 4, 7, 10 and 25), which matches E34. That confirms the formula should count where Month = 8 and Renewal = "New". I've kept the same absolute-reference style used in D30 and D31.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($E$1:$E$26,8,$B$1:$B$26,\"New\")"}

Column D ("Date") should hold the day number from A2. In "Fri 14 Mar 7:50pm", the day sits in characters 5–6, so the formula is `=MID(A2,5,2)`.

Some days may be single digits (for example "Sat 1 Mar"). To handle those, the formula takes the text between the first and second spaces instead of a fixed two characters. Adding `--` converts the result to a number.

{"formula": "=--MID(A2,5,FIND(\" \",A2,5)-5)"}

Each row in A:C is a pay period: column B is the start ("In (from)") and column A is the pay month. To find the pay month for the completed date in I3, look up I3 against the start dates in B and return the matching month from A. An approximate-match LOOKUP does this, since column B is in ascending order.

Some "Out" dates in column C look wrong (for example, 2011-01-22 in row 5), so the formula relies only on column B.

{"formula": "=LOOKUP(I3,$B$3:$B$41,$A$3:$A$41)"}

Column D is "Years Employed", and Y6 reads "Years Employed as of" with the reference date in Z6. So D6 should count the full years between the hire date in C6 and the date in Z6. DATEDIF with "Y" gives whole years.

Z6 is locked with `$Z$6` so the formula still points to the reference date when filled down the column.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(C6,$Z$6,\"Y\")"}

The worksheet fits a log-log model: column H predicts Ln Y as `Ln X * Slope + InterCept`. So for X = 1185 in N22, the forecast takes the log of N22, applies the slope and intercept, and converts back with `EXP`.

I've assumed `Slope` and `InterCept` are the named ranges column H already uses. N18 and N19 hold a different slope and intercept (1.00989 and 0.09304), possibly from the differenced regression. If you meant to forecast with those, the formula would need to change.

{"formula": "=EXP(LN(N22)*Slope+InterCept)"}

The "6x installment price" is most likely the payment due in each of the 6 installments. It is the amount whose present value at the discount rate equals the cash price for the period. In this formula, C6 is the discount rate, C7 is the number of payments, and D12 is the cash price.

This is a best guess, since the sheet doesn't spell it out. It assumes the 5% discount rate applies per installment period. The cash price is entered as negative so the payment comes out positive.

{"formula": "=PMT($C$6,$C$7,-D12)"}

C7 should give the total net rent over the lease, computed directly from the inputs rather than the proof-of-concept table in column I.

Net rent starts at C3−C4 and grows by CPI (C6) each year for C5 years. That is a geometric series, so the total is (C3−C4)·((1+C6)^C5−1)/C6. This should equal the I14 total.

{"formula": "=(C3-C4)*((1+C6)^C5-1)/C6"}

C32 should return the per-unit rate for the store-target section. That means looking up the units sold (B22) in the B26:G29 table, using the column whose header in row 24 (D24:G24) matches the % target met in B32 (0.9). The lookup on units is approximate, like the earlier VLOOKUPs.

B26:G29 has six columns: B, C, D, E, F, G. D24:G24 covers columns D–G, so MATCH returns 1 to 4 and adding 2 gives the right column index. With B32 = 0.9, MATCH gives 2, so the index is 4, which is column E. For 45 units, VLOOKUP lands on row 28 (min 40), giving 37.5. A 0.95 target would match 0.9 under approximate MATCH, which also seems right.

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(B22,$B$26:$G$29,MATCH(B32,$D$24:$G$24,1)+2,TRUE)"}

Row 22 computes the incremental (purchase) quantity as the MIN of rows 18 and 19, branching on the scheduling day code in AJ5. Row 23 is the decremental (sell) counterpart, so I mirrored C22's structure and swapped in rows 20 and 21.

Decremental values are negative or zero, so the natural counterpart of MIN would be MAX, which picks the smallest sell amount. I kept MIN as a direct copy of C22, but MAX may be what was intended.

{"formula": "=IF($AJ$5=6,IF(OR(C$11<7,C$11>22),0,MIN($I$21:$X$21)),IF($AJ$5=7,MIN($C$20:$Z$20),IF($AJ$5<4,IF(OR(C$11<7,C$11>22),MIN($C20:$H20,$Y20:$Z20),MIN($I20:$X20)),IF($AJ$5=4,IF(OR(C$11<7,C$11>22),MIN($C20:$H21,$X20:$Y21),MIN($I20:$X21)),IF($AJ$5=5,MIN($C20:$Z20,$Y21:$Z21,$C21:$H21),0)))))"}

Column E holds the interest for each month, so E10 should be the balance in B10 times the monthly rate. The PMT formulas in column C use a rate of 0.73109%.

That rate checks out against earlier rows: 1,650,000 × 0.0073109 ≈ 12,063, which matches E4.

{"formula": "=B10*0.73109%"}

B6 is labeled "lowest price" and the prices are in B2:F2, so the simplest fit is the minimum of that row: `=MIN(B2:F2)`.

Row 7 repeats the "lowest price" label, so B7 may be meant to show which supplier has that price.

{"formula": "=MIN(B2:F2)"}

G5 lines up with O5 on the right side, where the pattern is `=LARGE(I4:L4,COUNTIF(I4:L4,">"&K5)+1)`. Mapping that to the left table, G5 would most likely be `=LARGE(A4:D4,COUNTIF(A4:D4,">"&C5)+1)`.

The note in I1 asks for 0 instead of an error when no value is found. Wrapping the formula in `IFERROR(...,0)` handles that.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(LARGE(A4:D4,COUNTIF(A4:D4,\">\"&C5)+1),0)"}

E18 should hold a cubic fit of y against x. The fitted coefficients come back in order x³, x², x, intercept, and spill across E18:H18.

The existing values check out against this. F18 (−11) is the x² coefficient, G18 (19.83) is the x coefficient and H18 (−10) is the intercept. That gives an x³ coefficient of about 2.1667, which fits the points (1,1) and (2,3).

{"formula": "=LINEST(C18:C21,B18:B21^{1,2,3})"}

The "Match" column most likely wants the row position of the record matching the Start year (G10) and calendar week (H10). A MATCH on both conditions does this: it multiplies the two tests and finds the first row where both are true.

The I11 "end" row would use the same pattern. Note that in 2021 there is no week 3 in row 54; it is in row 56, so MATCH will still find it.

{"formula": "=MATCH(1,($B$3:$B$58=G10)*($A$3:$A$58=H10),0)"}

Q16 looks like it should hold Fund A's share of its 8,000 granted shares for the month in P16. The allocation is proportional to the days in that month: D3 × days in month ÷ total days in A's vesting period (start date E2 through end date M3). I used O16 for the monthly days. Its header says "Days (E:H)", but its formula is built on M$3 (Fund A's end date), so it fits A.

This simple pro‑rata may not match the existing allocation table exactly. For August 2020 it gives about 240.7, while D16 shows about 237.8. The original likely used a more piecewise method.

{"formula": "=$D$3*O16/($M$3-$E$2+1)"}

The label in A2 is "Highest Moving Average P" and C2 holds a window size of 15, so B2 most likely wants the largest 15-row moving average of column G (the P data).

This formula averages each 15-row window starting at G2 and takes the highest result. It uses the same SUBTOTAL/OFFSET style as D2. Unlike D2, it starts the windows at row 2, so the first window is included.

{"formula": "=MAX(SUBTOTAL(1,OFFSET(G2,ROW(G2:G310)-ROW(G2),,C2)))"}

The "Highest" column most likely takes the maximum value across the same Week 1–18 range that the Average column in T2 uses.

One caveat: these look like team rankings, where 1 is the best. If "Highest" means the best rank, `=MIN(B2:S2)` would be the right formula instead.

{"formula": "=MAX(B2:S2)"}

Model A's expected result is 2024-12. Its non-zero latest patches in M3:M8 are 2024-12, 2024-12 and 2025-01, so the smallest is 2024-12. Model B's expected result is 2024-10. Its non-zero values are 2024-12, 2024-10, 2024-11 and 2024-11, so the smallest is 2024-10.

The patch common to all countries is the smallest latest-patch date among countries where the model was actually patched. Since patches are cumulative, every country has at least that patch. Countries without the model return 0 from MAXIFS, so they are excluded with `">0"`.

{"formula": "=MINIFS(M3:M8,M3:M8,\">0\")"}

O6 should hold the full-year total for each product. The numbers confirm this: row 7 adds up to 34, matching O7. Row 6 adds up to 26, and 26 + 34 + 16 = 76, matching O9.

C9 already uses an `MMULT`/`SEQUENCE` formula that spills the column totals across D9:N9. O7 and O8 hold plain values, so O6 likely uses the same approach to sum each row and spill down into O7:O8.

{"formula": "=MMULT(--C6:N8,SEQUENCE(COLUMNS(C6:N8),1,1,0))"}

Row 11 is "Worked". Rows 13 and 14 subtract the time strings "08:00" and "12:00" from it, so it needs to be a time fraction rather than a number of hours.

The intended formula isn't certain here. Each day has two In/Out pairs (rows 7–8 and 9–10), but this column has only the first Time In (C7 = 09:00) and the second Time Out (C10 = 22:00). Summing two separate shifts would give a meaningless result with this data. So I treated the day as one span from first in to last out. `MOD(...,1)` handles shifts that run past midnight.

{"formula": "=MOD(C10-C7,1)"}

H8 holds the "Time" worked between the start time (F8) and end time (G8). I8 turns it into hours with `H8*24`, so H8 should be the time difference as a fraction of a day.

Some shifts can cross midnight, since other columns check `F>G` for night shifts. `MOD(G8-F8,1)` gives the correct duration in both cases. Wrapping it in `IF(G8="","",...)` keeps the cell blank when no end time has been entered.

{"formula": "=IF(G8=\"\",\"\",MOD(G8-F8,1))"}

D18 holds the number of months for the YTD column. The SUMPRODUCT formulas in column D use it as `D$18` to count back from the report month in D20.

YTD runs from the first month of the data (July 2022, in B4) up to D20 (December 2022). `DATEDIF` counts the whole months between those two dates, and adding 1 includes both ends, which gives 6 here.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF($B$4,D20,\"m\")+1"}

The column header says "TAT excluding weekends", which fits `NETWORKDAYS`. Turnaround time most likely runs from the Order Date (B) to the Completion Date (D).

The vacation dates in column F are free text, not real dates, so they can't be passed in as holidays. Row 2 has no vacation entry anyway, so the formula leaves that part out.

{"formula": "=NETWORKDAYS(B2,D2)"}

N2 should give the buy price implied by the yield in K2, so the PRICE function is the natural fit. It takes settlement D2, maturity F2, rate H2/100, yield K2, redemption 100, frequency G2 and basis 1. That matches the arguments used in K2's YIELD call.

PRICE returns a value per 100 face, so multiplying by B2/100 scales it to the bond's face value (FV). The result can then be compared directly with the buy price in C2.

{"formula": "=PRICE(D2,F2,H2/100,K2,100,G2,1)*B2/100"}

Total time-weighted return (TWR) is found by chaining the monthly returns: multiply (1 + each month's TWR) together, then subtract 1.

The monthly returns are in F6:F8, so the formula is the product of (1+F6:F8) minus 1. `SUMPRODUCT` isn't the right fit here, and `PRODUCT` over an array may need array entry in older Excel versions. Writing the factors out explicitly avoids that and works everywhere.

{"formula": "=(1+F6)*(1+F7)*(1+F8)-1"}

The label in F13 says "USING RATE FORMULA", so G13 should compute CAGR with RATE. That means RATE(nper, pmt, pv, fv).

The best candidate is the 3-year total in column F, since F10 holds 3 years. G10 is empty, so column G has no year count to use. The formula becomes =RATE(F10,0,-B7,F7), which should match F8.

{"formula": "=RATE(F10,0,-B7,F7)"}

M22 is in the "Desired Outcome" block and uses the SLN method. The rows above it (M19, M21) calculate accumulated depreciation as `=SLN(E,F,G)*I`. So the same pattern applies to row 22.

The printer was bought in 2009 with a 10-year useful life, so years to date is already past the useful life. Depreciation shouldn't continue beyond that point, which means the years multiplier should be capped at the useful life with `MIN(I22,G22)`. That cap is a deviation from the pattern in M19 and M21, but it follows the sheet's own rule that depreciation stops after the asset's useful life.

{"formula": "=SLN(E22,F22,G22)*MIN(I22,G22)"}

Column L holds the Hohmann transfer delta-V at point a: L3 = sqrt(mu/Ra)*(sqrt(2Rb/(Ra+Rb))-1).

M3 is the delta-V at point b, which uses the matching Hohmann formula: sqrt(mu/Rb)*(1-sqrt(2Ra/(Ra+Rb))).

{"formula": "=SQRT('Propulsion Subsystem Sizing'!$J$3/J3)*(1-SQRT(2*K3/(J3+K3)))"}

G5 is labelled "Annualised Volatility", and column D holds monthly returns in D2:D17. The standard approach is to take the sample standard deviation of the monthly returns and scale it by the square root of 12.

The dates run from 2021-03-31 to 2022-06-30 (rows 2–17), which matches the start and end dates in G2 and G3, so the full range D2:D17 is the right input.

{"formula": "=STDEV.S(D2:D17)*SQRT(12)"}

The "PayOff Balance" is most likely the ending balance (column E) of the last month that has an Actual Payment entered in column G. Because payments are entered month by month from the top, the number of filled payments gives the position of that month. The formula returns that row's balance.

E4 already computes a similar figure arithmetically, so this is a best guess at a separate approach.

{"formula": "=INDEX(E7:E82,COUNTIF(G7:G82,\">0\"))"}

I3 is "Total Work Hours", so I followed the same monthly SUMIFS pattern used in the neighbouring columns, which filter on dates in column G of 'WR Data'.

The hours column itself is an assumption. Column J on the summary sheet sums 'WR Data' column K, so I guessed that 'WR Data' column J holds the work hours. Column P is also unused and could be the hours column instead. Check which one it is before using this.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS('WR Data'!$J$3:$J$3996, 'WR Data'!$G$3:$G$3996, \">=\"&DATE($B$2, A3, 1), 'WR Data'!$G$3:$G$3996, \"<=\"&EOMONTH(DATE($B$2, A3, 1), 0))"}

Each row in this matrix starts in its first lower-triangle cell with a formula that transposes the upper-triangle values from the row above. For example, I31 contains `=TRANSPOSE(J30:K30)`.

Following that pattern, J32 should transpose the single cell K31. A plain reference to K31 gives the same result.

{"formula": "=TRANSPOSE(K31)"}

The header in H5 says "Trim Mean 10%" and the note in G4 says the only criterion is the task type. So H6 should take a 10% trimmed mean of the Time values (column C) for rows whose Task (column B) matches G6.

The formula wraps TRIMMEAN around an array IF that keeps only the matching rows. The ranges are absolute so the formula can be copied down to H7 and H8 for the other tasks.

{"formula": "=TRIMMEAN(IF($B$2:$B$61=G6,$C$2:$C$61),10%)"}

Yield to maturity can be computed with the YIELD function, using the settlement date, maturity date, coupon rate, price, face value, frequency and basis on the sheet.

YIELD expects the price per $100 of face value, so the value in B11 has to be scaled. The price argument is B11/B7*100, and the redemption is 100.

{"formula": "=YIELD(B2,B3,B5,B11/B7*100,100,B9,B10)"}

{"formula": "=COUNTA(UNIQUE(VSTACK(E2:E11,I2:I11)))"}

This counts the distinct dates across E2:E11 and I2:I11 combined, with each repeated date counted once. It needs Excel 365, because `VSTACK` and `UNIQUE` aren't available in older versions.

The header could also mean counting only the dates that appear exactly once. If so, use `UNIQUE(...,,TRUE)` instead.

The H:I columns hold a lookup table that maps state names to abbreviations. D2 most likely needs a combined code: the state abbreviation followed by the first letter of the status and the product.

If that's the intent, this formula looks up the abbreviation and appends the initials:

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(A2,$H$2:$I$4,2,FALSE)&\"-\"&LEFT(B2,1)&\"-\"&C2"}

A simpler alternative would be just the abbreviation. The combined code is a guess, so I'll return only the plain lookup:

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(A2,$H$2:$I$4,2,FALSE)"}

The cell sits under the "2024 YTD High" label, so it most likely holds the highest balance across the 2024 date columns. Row 10 holds the daily totals, and V2:Y2 are the 2024 dates. Z is 2023-12-29, so it is excluded.

The user may add more columns to the right later, but the existing columns V:Y are what I'll use.

{"formula": "=MAX(V10:Y10)"}

F3 returns the old-data Easting (column L) of the point nearest to the new point in B3/C3. Nearest means the smallest straight-line distance using both coordinates. E3 then matches that Easting in column L to get the old GULLY_NO.

The formula compares squared distances, which gives the same nearest point without needing a square root. It relies on array evaluation, so older Excel versions may need Ctrl+Shift+Enter. Two points with the same Easting would make E3's lookup return the first match.

{"formula": "=INDEX($L$3:$L$21,MATCH(MIN(($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2),($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2,0))"}

The column I header is "hours in working days in the middle of the period". The matching piece of the one-cell formula in L2 is the last term: `MAX(0,NETWORKDAYS(WORKDAY(E,1),WORKDAY(F,-1))*(22-8))`.

Replacing the long nested expressions with the helper columns E2 (corrected start) and F2 (corrected finish) gives the formula below. It counts the working days strictly between the first and last day, then multiplies by the 14 working hours per day (8:00 to 22:00).

{"formula": "=MAX(0,NETWORKDAYS(WORKDAY(E2,1),WORKDAY(F2,-1))*(22-8))"}

X12 is labelled "Total Pips Won/Avg", and Y12 already holds the total pips won (`=SUMIF(T6:T203,">0")`). So Z12 should be the average pips per winning trade.

Z8 holds the count of winning trades (`=COUNTIF(U6:U203,">0")`), so dividing Y12 by Z8 gives that average.

{"formula": "=Y12/Z8"}

M10 is the "QC % Per Tech" column. It most likely measures QC completed against jobs taken to QC, so the base calculation is L10/K10.

I followed the guarded division style already used in column J (completion %). That pattern returns 0 instead of an error when the inputs are zero, which matters for row 13 where K and L are both 0.

{"formula": "=IF(AND(K10<>0,L10<>0),L10/K10,0)"}

B39 is the overs total for Baxter. C39 sums column B (maidens), so overs must come from column A. Overs are in cricket notation, where .1 means one ball, so a plain SUM would be wrong. The decimal part has to be counted as balls and carried over at 6.

The approach: total balls = INT(overs)×6 + decimal×10. Then convert back to overs notation with INT(total/6) + MOD(total,6)/10. The ×10 can leave floating-point fractions, so the ball count is wrapped in ROUND.

{"formula": "=INT(SUMPRODUCT(ROUND(INT(A2:A37)*6+(A2:A37-INT(A2:A37))*10,0))/6)+MOD(SUMPRODUCT(ROUND(INT(A2:A37)*6+(A2:A37-INT(A2:A37))*10,0)),6)/10"}

E8 is labelled "avg cost (excl outliers)". The outlier limits are already set up: E5 is the low limit and E6 is the high limit. So E8 should average the per-unit costs in C2:C15 that fall between those two limits.

E9 ("all data") is probably a quantity-weighted average, but E8 most naturally maps to AVERAGEIFS with the two limit conditions.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIFS(C2:C15,C2:C15,\">=\"&E5,C2:C15,\"<=\"&E6)"}

Row 5 ("Expected Results") appears to be the row 3 value with leading spaces added. Row 1 ("Blank cells before text") holds how many spaces to add for each column. So the formula repeats a space B1 times and joins it to B3.

The sample data doesn't match this exactly in every column; F5, for example, shows 3000 with no visible padding.

{"formula": "=REPT(\" \",B1)&B3"}

D8 sits in the CAGR row under the YEAR 1 column, and D10 holds the year count (1). So D8 should be the portfolio's compound annual growth from the starting total (B7) to the Year 1 total (D7) over D10 years: `(D7/B7)^(1/D10)-1`.

Writing it as `($B7)` keeps the start column fixed and lets the formula fill right across the other years.

{"formula": "=(D7/$B7)^(1/D10)-1"}

Row 9 is the "less-precise trend" row, and M9:O9 hold quadratic coefficients for x^2, x^1 and x^0. So B9 should evaluate that polynomial at the event value in B2. The coefficient references are absolute so the formula can be filled across to K9.

Quick check at x=10: 0.18 + 1.29 − 0.64 = 0.83, which is close to Autobrake 1 (0.6).

{"formula": "=$M$9*B2^2+$N$9*B2+$O$9"}
